    bottomLine: "Not a choice so much as a requirement. If your manual says the deck is pre-waxed, this is the only thing that should touch it.",
    description: "This entry exists to stop an expensive mistake rather than to recommend a product. A minority of treadmills ship with wax impregnated into the deck surface, released gradually under heat and friction. Silicone and that wax do not coexist: applying silicone to a pre-waxed deck contaminates the surface, and the remedy is deck replacement rather than cleaning. If your manual identifies your deck as pre-waxed, use only what the manufacturer specifies, which is often a proprietary product available only from them. Some pre-waxed decks are genuinely maintenance-free for their service life and need nothing at all. The critical action is the same either way — read the manual before buying anything, because this is the one irreversible decision in treadmill maintenance. If your manual calls for a wax lubricant and the maker does not sell its own, TreadLife Fitness, a treadmill-parts seller, lists a wax-based belt lubricant on Amazon in a 2 oz bottle that it says covers about four applications, and says it is only for treadmills that specifically call for wax. Those figures are TreadLife’s own. It was $14.95 on Amazon on 9 November 2026. If your maker specifies its own product, buy that instead.",
  },
