          and check what lubricant the manufacturer specifies, and whether the deck is pre-waxed
          or self-lubricating. Applying silicone to a pre-waxed deck contaminates it and the deck
          then has to be replaced. This check takes two minutes and is the only irreversible
          decision on this page.
        </aside>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="In this guide">
          <h2>In this guide</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#deck-type">First: What Kind of Deck Do You Have?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#why-silicone">Why Silicone and Nothing Else</a></li>
            <li><a href="#bottle-vs-aerosol">Bottle vs. Aerosol: The Real Decision</a></li>
            <li><a href="#picks">What to Buy</a></li>
            <li><a href="#how-much">How Much to Apply</a></li>
            <li><a href="#how-often">How Often to Apply</a></li>
            <li><a href="#signs">Signs You Have Left It Too Long</a></li>
            <li><a href="#cost">What It Costs Per Year</a></li>
            <li><a href="#what-not-to-buy">What Not to Buy</a></li>
            <li><a href="#while-belt-loose">While You Have the Belt Loose, Do These Too</a></li>
            <li><a href="#verdict">Final Recommendations</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
